op","name":"Will Alsop","time":518.88,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"}]},"An American in Paris":{"slug":"an-american-in-paris","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Three friends struggle to find work in Paris. However, things become more complicated when two of them fall in love with the same woman.<b></b></p>","subtitle":"Film","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/an-american-in-paris.jpg","external":"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0043278/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Paris,+France/@48.856614,2.3522219,7z","also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1250.36,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Baron Philippe de Rothschild":{"slug":"baron-philippe-de-rothschild","body":"Baron Philippe de Rothschild (13 April 1902 – 20 January 1988) was a member of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rothschild_family\">Rothschild</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rothschild_banking_family_of_France\">banking dynasty</a> who became a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Prix_motor_racing\">Grand Prix</a> race-car driver, a screenwriter and playwright, a theatrical producer, a film producer, a poet, and one of the most successful wine growers in the world.","subtitle":"Rothschild dynasty","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/baron-philippe-de-rothschild.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philippe_de_Rothschild","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Paris,+France/@48.8622759,2.3365501,13z","also":[{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":125.6,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"BD":{"slug":"bd","body":"Building Design, or BD, is a weekly <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture\">architectural</a> magazine and digital title in the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom\">United Kingdom</a>. BD was launched in 1970 by publisher Morgan Grampian as a closed circulation weekly as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-tech_architecture\">high-tech architecture</a> was just starting to take-off. It ceased its print edition in March 2014, remaining a digital only publication. The title soon carved a special niche for itself because of the extent of its news coverage, range of job adverts as well as its campaigning voice. Its tabloid format and news based content has earned it the reputation as one of the UK's best business titles leading to a clutch of industry awards. It is the most requested title in the library of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Institute_of_British_Architects\">Royal Institute of British Architects</a>. Unlike most other architectural publications BD’s editors, with a few exceptions, are journalists not architects.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":624.08,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":593.04,"topicId":"generator","topicLabel":"GENERATOR"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":275.88,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":851.52,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1500.24,"topicId":"communications","topicLabel":"Communications"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":188.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Buckminster Fuller":{"slug":"buckminster-fuller","body":"Richard Buckminster \"Bucky\" Fuller published more than 30 books, coining or popularizing terms such as \"<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spaceship_Earth\">Spaceship Earth</a>\", <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephemeralization\">ephemeralization</a>, and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synergetics_(Fuller)\">synergetic</a>. He also developed numerous inventions, mainly architectural designs, and popularized the widely known <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geodesic_dome\">geodesic dome</a>. Carbon molecules known as <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fullerene\">fullerenes</a> were later named by scientists for their resemblance to geodesic spheres. Buckminster Fuller was the second president of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mensa_International\">Mensa</a> from 1974 to 1983.","subtitle":"Neo-futuristic architect, systems theorist, author, designer, and inventor.","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/buckminster-fuller.jpg","external":"https://bfi.org","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Milton,+MA,+USA/@42.2495321,-71.0661653,7z","also":[{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":438.2,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"hans-ulrich-obrist","name":"Hans Ulrich Obrist","time":1350.68,"topicId":"institutions","topicLabel":"Institutions"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":123.44,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":549.32,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1393.16,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"}]},"CCA":{"slug":"cca","body":"The Canadian Centre for Architecture (CCA) was founded in 1979 as a new form of cultural institution to build public awareness of the role of architecture in society, to promote scholarly research in the field, and to stimulate innovation in design practice.\r\n\r\nThe CCA holds one of the world’s foremost international research collections of publications, conceptual studies, drawings, plans, models, prints, photographs, architectural archives, related artefacts and ephemera. Over 50 years ago, architect Phyllis Lambert, Founding Director Emeritus, began the collection that would become the cornerstone of the institution. Today the CCA Collection, comprising works dating from the Renaissance to the present day, documents the culture of architecture worldwide.\r\n\r\nUnparalleled in scope, the 100,000 prints and drawings, more than 60,000 photographs, 150 archives, 215,000 volumes, and over 5,000 periodical titles comprise dynamically interrelated bodies of primary and secondary materials that advance thinking about the nature of the built domain and the ideas that underlie it. The guiding purpose of the Collection is to make comprehensive and integrated bodies of material available for advanced, interdisciplinary research in order to reveal the changing character of thought and observation pertaining to the built world, provide evidence in depth of cultural and intellectual circles of the past, and point to the future of architectural thinking and practice.","subtitle":"Canadian Centre for Architecture","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/cca.jpg","external":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/294-institutional-overview","location":"http://www.cca.qc.ca/en/collection/294-institutional-overview","also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":806.12,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":1384.16,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":8.32,"topicId":"englishness","topicLabel":"Englishness"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":838.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Cedric Price Office":{"slug":"cedric-price-office","body":"","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":444.04,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1751.88,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":43.4,"topicId":"drawing","topicLabel":"Drawing"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":1.64,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":314.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":442.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":184.12,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":247.32,"topicId":"pop-up-parliament","topicLabel":"POP UP PARLIAMENT"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1065.2,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1063.84,"topicId":"conscience","topicLabel":"Conscience"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":272.16,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":181.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":466.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":26.88,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":716.28,"topicId":"potteries-thinkbelt","topicLabel":"POTTERIES THINKBELT"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":743.6,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"steve-mullin","name":"Steve Mullin","time":1639.24,"topicId":"housing-research","topicLabel":"HOUSING RESEARCH"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":2.64,"topicId":"interaction-centre","topicLabel":"INTERACTION CENTRE"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":568.32,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":672,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":761.16,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"Cedric Price Works":{"slug":"cedric-price-works","body":"Published in two volumes, and in conjunction with the Canadian Centre for Architecture (CCA), Montreal, this forthcoming publication will bring together for the first time all of the projects (volume one) and writings (volume two) by British architect Cedric Price (1934–2003). A chronological arrangement will place the work in the context of the immediate postwar period in England in which Price grew up, illuminating how cultural, social and political factors conditioned the work from the outset, and then subsequently shaped its development as his practice changed throughout the 1960s, 70s, 80s and 90s. Full project descriptions will be set alongside a careful selection of illustrations, many previously unpublished – from conceptual sketches through to presentation drawings and related artefacts. The content material is drawn directly from the original work, now largely held in the Cedric Price Fonds at the CCA, as well as a few private collections – the culmination of ten years of study by author/editor Samantha Hardingham. The books aim to present the munificence of Price: thinker, philosopher, artist and unparalleled raconteur – a thoroughly modern-traditionalist.","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":451.12,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":853.56,"topicId":"present","topicLabel":"Present"},{"slug":"thomas-weaver","name":"Thomas Weaver","time":786.8,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Clip Kit":{"slug":"clip-kit","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Clip Kit was a student magazine at the Architectural Association, initiated by peter Murray.<span style=\"font-family: 'Calibri','sans-serif'; mso-ascii-theme-font: major-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: 'MS Mincho'; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-fareast; mso-hansi-theme-font: major-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: Helvetica; color: black; mso-themecolor: text1; mso-bidi-font-weight: bold;\"> </span></p>","subtitle":"By Peter Murray","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/clip-kit.jpg","external":"http://books.google.nl/books?id=9z_zrQLuY3kC&amp;pg=PA442&amp;lpg=PA442&amp;dq=clip+kit+peter+murray&amp;source=bl&amp;ots=5mU768GCgA&amp;sig=25mQWjjq6cBEQJmPd5RStBKSMko&amp;hl=en&amp;sa=X&amp;ei=8Jh1VK2IGtXraqeVgkg&amp;ved=0CDQQ6AEwBA#v=onepage&amp;q=clip%20kit%20peter%20murray&amp;f=false","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/aa+school+of+architecture/@51.518231,-0.130927,15z","also":[{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":317.36,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Cyd Charisse":{"slug":"cyd-charisse","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Cyd Charisse (March 8, 1922 – June 17, 2008) was an American actress and dancer.After recovering from <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polio\">polio</a> as a child, and studying ballet, Charisse entered films in the 1940s. Her roles usually focused on her abilities as a dancer, and she was paired with <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Astaire\">Fred Astaire</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gene_Kelly\">Gene Kelly</a>; her films include <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singin%27_in_the_Rain\">Singin' in the Rain</a> (1952), <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Band_Wagon\">The Band Wagon</a> (1953) and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk_Stockings_(film)\">Silk Stockings</a> (1957). She stopped dancing in films in the late 1950s, but continued acting in film and television, and in 1992 made her <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadway_theatre\">Broadway</a> debut. In her later years, she discussed the history of the Hollywood musical in documentaries, and was featured in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/That%27s_Entertainment!_III\">That's Entertainment! III</a> in 1994. She was awarded the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Medal_of_Arts\">National Medal of the Arts and Humanities</a> in 2006.</p>","subtitle":"Actress and dancer","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/cyd-charisse.jpg","external":"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyd_Charisse","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Los+Angeles,+CA,+USA/@34.0522342,-118.2436849,7z","also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1248.76,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Edward Wadie Said":{"slug":"edward-wadie-said","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\">Edward Wadie Said, 1 November 1935 – 25 September 2003) was a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palestinian_American\">Palestinian American</a> literary theorist and public intellectual who helped found the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical-theory\">critical-theory</a> field of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Postcolonialism\">postcolonialism</a>. Born a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palestinian_people\">Palestinian</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerusalem\">Jerusalem</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mandatory_Palestine\">Mandatory Palestine</a>, he was an American citizen through his father. Said spent his childhood in Jerusalem and Cairo, where he attended elite British and American schools. Subsequently he left for the United States, where he obtained a bachelor's degree from <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princeton_University\">Princeton</a> and a doctorate in English literature from <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_University\">Harvard</a>. Said then joined the faculty of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbia_University\">Columbia University</a> in 1963, where he became professor of English and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparative_literature\">comparative literature</a> in 1991.</p>","subtitle":null,"image":null,"external":null,"location":null,"also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1224.92,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Eleanor Bron":{"slug":"eleanor-bron","body":"Eleanor Bron (born 14 March 1938) is an English stage, film and television actress and author. Bron was the partner of the architect <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Price\">Cedric Price</a> for many years until his death in 2003; they had no children.","subtitle":"Actress and author","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/eleanor-bron.jpg","external":"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0111376/","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/London,+UK/@51.5286416,-0.1015987,11z","also":[{"slug":"carlos-villanueva-brandt","name":"Carlos Villanueva Brandt","time":1038.72,"topicId":"delight","topicLabel":"Delight"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1649,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":643.88,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":189.76,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Learning from Las Vegas":{"slug":"learning-from-las-vegas","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Learning from Las Vegas created a healthy controversy on its appearance in 1972, calling for architects to be more receptive to the tastes and values of \"common\" people and less immodest in their erections of \"heroic,\" self-aggrandizing monuments. This revision includes the full texts of Part I of the original, on the Las Vegas strip, and Part II, \"Ugly and Ordinary Architecture, or the Decorated Shed,\" a generalization from the findings of the first part on symbolism in architecture and the iconography of urban sprawl. (The final part of the first edition, on the architectural work of the firm Venturi and Rauch, is not included in the revision.) The new paperback edition has a smaller format, fewer pictures, and a considerably lower price than the original. There are an added preface by Scott Brown and a bibliography of writings by the members of Venturi and Rauch and about the firm's work.</p>","subtitle":"Robert Venturi, Denise Scott Brown and Steven Izenour","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/learning-from-las-vegas.jpg","external":"http://mitpress.mit.edu/books/learning-las-vegas","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Las+Vegas,+NV,+USA/@36.1699412,-115.1398296,7z","also":[{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1268.52,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Price’s candles":{"slug":"prices-candles","body":"<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Price's Candles, founded in 1830, is an importer and retailer of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Candle\">candles</a>. Its full name is Price's Patent Candles Ltd. The firm is headquartered in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bedford\">Bedford</a> and holds the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Warrant\">Royal Warrant</a> for the supply of candles. It is now the largest candle supplier in the UK. They hold an important place in the technological <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_candle_making\">history of candle making</a>.</p>","subtitle":"Factory","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/prices-candles.jpg","external":"http://www.prices-candles.co.uk","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Bedford,+UK/@52.1390923,-0.4515815,13z","also":[{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":114.2,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"Retriever":{"slug":"retriever","body":"In 2004 Eleanor Bron and Samantha Hardingham set about locating and cataloguing each title from Cedric Price’s personal library. What has emerged is a map of Price’s thought processes and imagination – a set of both strategic and unruly co-ordinates for the projects that emerged from his office.\r\n\r\nBron and Hardingham, in keeping with the coding system of Cedric Price Architects, have created an annotated catalogue with symbols and codes referring to possible categories in which to place each book. However, space is left for the reader to add notes and annotations and the utilitarian nature of the publication – offered as a self-assembly kit – allows readers to assemble it as they wish and create their own map from the catalogue. A4 size, the pages fit into any binding system.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n<span lang=\"EN-GB\"> </span>","subtitle":"By Eleanor Bron and Samantha Hardingham","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/retriever.jpg","external":"http://www.cornerhouse.org/bookstore/product/cedric-price-retriever","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/One+Alfred+Place,+1+Alfred+Pl,+Fitzrovia,+London+WC1E+7EB,+UK/@51.5196073,-0.1326004,17z","also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":524.84,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"john-frazer","name":"John Frazer","time":1653.32,"topicId":"thinking","topicLabel":"Thinking"},{"slug":"paul-finch","name":"Paul Finch","time":1482.68,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":516.84,"topicId":"time","topicLabel":"Time"}]},"Richard Meier":{"slug":"richard-meier","body":"Richard Meier (born October 12, 1934) is an American <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abstract_art\">abstract artist</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a>, whose geometric designs make prominent use of the color white. A winner of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pritzker_Architecture_Prize\">Pritzker Architecture Prize</a> in 1984, some of his more iconic buildings include the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barcelona_Museum_of_Contemporary_Art\">Barcelona Museum of Contemporary Art</a> and the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Getty_Center\">Getty Center</a> in <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles\">Los Angeles</a>.","subtitle":"Architect","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/richard-meier.jpg","external":"http://www.richardmeier.com","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/475+10th+Ave,+New+York,+NY+10018,+USA/@40.7562375,-73.9984038,17z","also":[{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":1092.64,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]},"The White Room":{"slug":"the-white-room","body":"Cedric Price’s famous all-white room, located at the top of an Edwardian building in Alfred Place, just off Tottenham Court Road in London, was sparsely furnished - just a couple of chairs and a desk, on which stands an Anglepoise lamp, an ashtray and a decanter of brandy. As you toiled up the stairs you pass notices - \"Wipe your feet!\" and (on the next landing) \"And again!\". The all-white room is not just a bolt-hole where the famously sociable Price retires to think and smoke one of his favourite cigars: it was a power-house of radical ideas.\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-right: 53.5pt; tab-stops: 0in;\"></p>","subtitle":"CP Office","image":null,"external":"http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/4708428/Think-the-unimaginable.html","location":"https://maps.google.com/?q=38+Alfred+Pl,+London+WC1E,+UK","also":[{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1639.64,"topicId":"food-and-drink","topicLabel":"Food and drink"},{"slug":"jeremy-melvin","name":"Jeremy Melvin","time":1750.04,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"max-neal","name":"Max Neal","time":388.44,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"paul-barker","name":"Paul Barker","time":1136.04,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":283.72,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"},{"slug":"peter-murray","name":"Peter Murray","time":1046.08,"topicId":"network","topicLabel":"Network"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":213.96,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"samantha-hardingham","name":"Samantha Hardingham","time":470.36,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":577.76,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"},{"slug":"will-alsop","name":"Will Alsop","time":877.08,"topicId":"office","topicLabel":"Office"}]},"Walter Gropius":{"slug":"walter-gropius","body":"Walter Adolph Georg Gropius (18 May 1883 – 5 July 1969) was a <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germans\">German</a><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architect\">architect</a> and founder of the <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bauhaus\">Bauhaus</a> School, who, along with <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludwig_Mies_van_der_Rohe\">Ludwig Mies van der Rohe</a>, <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Le_Corbusier\">Le Corbusier</a> and <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Lloyd_Wright\">Frank Lloyd Wright</a>, is widely regarded as one of the pioneering masters of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modern_architecture\">modern architecture</a>.","subtitle":"Founder of the Bauhaus School","image":"/cedric-price-memory-bank/cards/walter-gropius.jpg","external":"http://bauhaus-online.de/en/atlas/personen/walter-gropius","location":"https://www.google.com/maps/place/Cambridge,+MA,+USA/@42.3736158,-71.1097335,7z","also":[{"slug":"brett-steele","name":"Brett Steele","time":220.32,"topicId":"personality","topicLabel":"Personality"}]}}}